# Copy to .env.local and fill in.
#
# This file IS committed — .gitignore covers `.env*` but makes an exception for
# it, because it is the documentation for everything below. `.env.local` is not
# committed, which is where real values go. Never put a token in here.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Getting in
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The door asks for two things: an email address and a password.
#
# The password is the gate, and it is printed inside the sponsored copy. Every
# reader types the same word; there are no accounts to sign in to and nothing
# to be approved for.
#
# Be clear-eyed about what it is. It is printed, in circulation, and identical
# in every copy of the run, so anyone who photographs the page can pass on what
# they saw. That is inherent to a static print run, and it is why nothing that
# matters hangs on it: the reader password can never administer the library no
# matter who types it.
SITE_PASSWORD=Exium

# The administrator's password. This one is NOT printed anywhere, and on its
# own it is not enough: see ADMIN_EMAILS below.
#
# Note the shape: the admin password is the reader password plus a character.
# That is how the sponsor wanted it, and it is why `lib/auth/config.ts`
# compares the admin password FIRST and with an exact `===`: a prefix match
# or a reader-first test would hand /admin to every reader.
ADMIN_PASSWORD=Exiumm

# The addresses that administer the library — one or more, separated by commas
# (semicolons and whitespace work too, because a value pasted into a hosting
# dashboard picks up whichever the person happened to type).
#
#   ADMIN_EMAILS=librarian@example.org,second-account@example.org
#
# BOTH halves are required for /admin: the address must be on this list AND the
# admin password must be typed. Neither alone is enough, and an address here
# that types the *reader* password is an ordinary reader for that session.
#
# Compared — case-insensitively, trimmed — against the address inside the
# signed session, on every admin page, every admin Server Action and in the
# route guard. It is read from the environment each time rather than stamped
# into the cookie, so removing an address takes effect on the next request
# rather than when an eight-hour session expires.
#
# Left blank, NOBODY is an administrator. That is deliberate: an unset variable
# must not match a blank address and hand /admin to the next visitor.
#
# `ADMIN_EMAIL` (singular) is still read when this is unset — it is the older
# name for the same setting.
ADMIN_EMAILS=

# The address typed by an ordinary reader grants nothing and is checked against
# nothing; it is recorded so the sponsor can see the print run is being read.
# See the Redis section at the bottom — without it, that record is not durable.

# Session cookie signing key. Any long random string:
#   openssl rand -base64 48
# Rotating it signs everyone out. Required in production; development falls
# back to a fixed insecure value so a fresh clone can be opened locally.
AUTH_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The book files
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PDFs are not in public/: a file with an extension is skipped by the proxy's
# matcher, which would make the gate on the pages decoration. They are served
# by `app/api/file/[slug]/route.ts`, which checks the same signed cookie and
# supports range requests.
#
# In development that route reads `private/books/<slug>.pdf` straight off disk.
# In production, point this at a GitHub Release (or R2 bucket) holding the same
# slug-named files and the route proxies the bytes instead.
#
#   BOOKS_RELEASE_BASE=https://github.com/<owner>/<repo>/releases/download/v1.0-books
BOOKS_RELEASE_BASE=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Upstash Redis
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REQUIRED IN PRODUCTION. Three things use it, and all three degrade badly
# without it on a serverless host, where the filesystem is per-instance and
# wiped by every deploy:
#
#   • The register (/signup)      — falls back to private/readers.json
#   • The door record (/signin)   — falls back to private/accounts.json
#   • The door's rate limit       — falls back to a Map inside one instance,
#                                   so the ten-attempt limit becomes ten per
#                                   lambda rather than ten per address
#
# Either pair of names works; `KV_*` is what Vercel's own KV integration sets
# and `UPSTASH_*` is what the Upstash console shows. Set one pair.
#
# Do not paste a token into this file: it is committed. Set them in the Vercel
# dashboard, or in .env.local, which is not.
KV_REST_API_URL=
KV_REST_API_TOKEN=
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL=
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN=
